# Copy to `.env` and edit. docker compose reads it automatically, so the values
# here save repeating them on every `docker compose --profile api up`.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored: everything in it is about *your* machine, and one of the
# two settings below is a deliberate trust decision that should not travel with
# the repository.

# The repository the API manages, mounted at the same path inside the container
# and passed through to -project. Defaults to `$PWD`, which is right only when
# you launch from the repository root — compose finds docker-compose.yml by
# walking *up* from where you are, so `docker compose --profile api up` in a
# subdirectory like `studio/` otherwise hands the API that subdirectory, whose
# `.git` is one level outside the mount. Every branch-addressed request then
# fails with "not a git repository".
#
# Set it to the repository root and the directory you launch from stops
# mattering.
SANDBOX_PROJECT=/absolute/path/to/your/repository

# The config file the API loads, passed through to -config.
#
# Needed when this project has a `.sandbox.yaml` carrying keys a project config
# may not — image, mounts, secrets, env_allow, or a network.mode that weakens
# what is in force. Discovery refuses those and the server will not start, which
# is correct: that file travels with the repository and anyone who clones it
# gets whatever it says.
#
# Naming the path is the deliberate act that makes it trusted, which is the
# escape hatch the refusal itself describes. Read the file before you do this.
# Leave it unset and discovery behaves normally.
SANDBOX_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/your/.sandbox.yaml

# Required on every request but /v1/health when set.
#
# Strongly recommended if you run the api profile at all: without it, anything
# that can reach 127.0.0.1:8787 can launch a container — and the API container
# holds the docker socket, so "launch a container" means "with any mount it
# likes". Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# SANDBOX_STUDIO_TOKEN=
